Teachers often work longer hours and take on additional responsibilities, such as mentoring and extracurricular activities, without additional compensation.
The average salary for teachers is significantly lower than that of professionals in other fields such as law, medicine, and engineering, despite similar levels of education and responsibility.
Many countries with strong education systems pay teachers competitively, showing that it is possible to attract and retain qualified educators without sacrificing their financial well-being.
Underpayment of teachers can lead to higher turnover rates and a shortage of qualified educators, negatively impacting student learning outcomes.
Investing in fair compensation for teachers can improve morale, reduce burnout, and ultimately enhance the quality of education provided to students.